+## Project checkers (`project-checks`)
+
+`linter` and `type-check` assume a TypeScript project with ESLint and `tsc`.
+`project-checks` runs whatever the project lists in
+`aidlc/spaces/<space>/checkers.json` instead:
+
+```json
+{
+  "schemaVersion": 1,
+  "checkers": [
+    { "label": "ruff", "command": [".venv/bin/ruff", "check", "--output-format=json", "{file}"], "matches": "**/*.py", "parser": "ruff" },
+    { "label": "tsc", "command": ["node_modules/.bin/tsc", "--noEmit", "--pretty", "false"], "matches": "src/**/*.ts", "parser": "generic", "timeoutSeconds": 60 },
+    { "label": "unit tests", "command": ["bun", "test"], "matches": "src/**/*.ts", "parser": "exit-code" }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `label` | Name shown in findings |
+| `command` | argv array, never a shell string; `{file}` becomes the written file's path relative to `cwd`, and a command without it checks the whole project |
+| `cwd` | Optional project-relative working directory; its `node_modules/.bin` and `.venv/bin` are searched before `PATH` |
+| `matches` | Optional project-relative glob (default: every file) |
+| `parser` | `eslint` (`--format=json`), `ruff` (`--output-format=json`), `generic` (`file:line:col: severity: message` and `tsc --pretty false` lines; the default) or `exit-code` (a non-zero exit is one finding carrying the output's last 30 lines) |
+| `timeoutSeconds` | Optional, default 30; all checkers share the manifest's 120-second budget |
+
+An error-severity finding fails the fire; warnings are listed only. A failing
+run whose output the parser cannot read is itself a finding, so a broken
+checker never reads as a clean pass. A missing executable, a `cwd` outside the
+project and a timeout are reported in the verdict's `checkers` list without
+failing. With no `checkers.json`, or no checker matching the file, the fire
+passes with `Note=tool-unavailable`; a malformed file is a script error.
+
+Try the configuration without a workflow:
+`aidlc engine sensor-project-checks --stage code-generation --file-path <file>`.
+On Coding Harness, `/aidlc-checkers` finds the project's commands and writes the file.
 
 ---
 
